# ============================================================================
# ACCOUNTED-FOR SECRET-SCAN FINDINGS
# ============================================================================
#
# Every entry here is a finding the full-history audit reports that we have
# looked at and decided about. CLAUDE.md's Secrets law says the scan has no
# allowlist and that a false positive is the owner's deliberate decision, not a
# default a session sets. This file is where those decisions are written down,
# which is what keeps them deliberate.
#
# THE FILE IS EMPTY OF ENTRIES ON PURPOSE. It lands with the convention and the
# test, and entries are added only after the audit has actually run and its
# findings have been read. Writing entries first would be deciding about
# findings nobody has seen.
#
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE CONVENTION: THE REASON GOES ON THE LINE ABOVE THE FINGERPRINT.
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
#   # <why this finding is accounted for, in a sentence>
#   <fingerprint>
#
# Not a trailing comment on the fingerprint line. Whether gitleaks treats
# everything after a `#` on an entry line as a comment or as part of the
# fingerprint was not established, and a convention that depends on an
# unverified parsing detail fails silently -- the entry simply stops matching
# and the finding comes back, or worse, matches nothing and is never noticed.
# The line above parses safely whichever way trailing text is handled.
#
# `tests/gitleaksignore-reasons.test.ts` enforces it: an entry with no reason
# above it fails the suite. An unexplained new finding is red; a known and
# explained old one is not.
#
# A fingerprint is `commit:file:rule:line` and carries no secret value.
